Check if one string is a rotation of another; 
Why check len(a) == len(b) first?
Think about what rotation actually means — you're just moving letters from front to back, nothing is added or removed. 
So a rotation of a will always have the exact same length as a.
Take a = "waterbottle", b = "erbottlewat" (rotated by 3). If you just search b in a, you're asking: does "erbottlewat" appear as a continuous stretch inside "waterbottle"? It doesn't — the letters wrap around from the end back to the beginning, so in the original string alone, it's broken into two separate pieces at opposite ends.
By doubling the string (a+a), you recreate that "wrap-around" continuity:
a+a = "waterbottle" + "waterbottle" = "waterbottlewaterbottle"
Now the seam is "healed" — any rotation of a, no matter where you cut it, will always show up as one unbroken stretch somewhere inside a+a. That's the whole trick — doubling turns a circular problem into a straight-line search problem.
a = input("Enter first string")
b = input("Enter second string")
if len(a) == len(b) and b in (a + a):
    print("Rotation")
else:
    print("Not a rotation")
